A [feature flag](/systems/flags) declares a ladder of values.
Each rung names its authority and the evidence required to leave it.
A climb moves exactly one rung. A downward move uses `off`.

## Measure the exit where the flag is read

Every exit names an event, a measurement location, and a threshold.
Record the observation through `record_flag_evidence`, with its source reference.

A read scope is the partition for a flag value and its evidence.
The child's `readScope` [schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary) defines the flag's scope. Its default is the literal `global` scope.
A measurement counts only at its own scope and current rung.

A flip clears current evidence. Returning to a rung needs fresh measurements.

A mismatch returns `FlagScopeMismatch` and a repair instruction.
The same scope check applies to readers, evidence, and flips.

## Authority comes from approval

The roles are `agent`, `arbiter`, and `owner`.
The child supplies the [capability approval service](/systems/capabilities).
That service authorizes the requested climb. Token checks stay in the child's composition.

A climb above agent also needs an `approvalRef` quote.
A role field alone grants nothing.

`off` still passes through the shared capability implementation and attempt audit.
It supplies an always-granted approval implementation, so an approval outage cannot block it.
The flag's declared `kill` policy selects its destination or explains why no stop exists.

## Commit once, then project

A projection is a copy derived from a committed value.

The Durable Object owns the current value and its receipt.
It commits both with a dirty projection marker in one transaction.
An alarm writes that committed value to the KV read replica.

```text
operator → Pending → approval → DO value + receipt + dirty marker
                                                    │
                                                    ▼
                                             alarm → KV → reader
```

The Pending attempt exists before the approval gate.
A separate outcome records success, denial, or failure.
An interrupted call can remain Pending. The authoritative position can still show a committed receipt.

A crash before projection leaves the dirty marker intact.
Redrive uses the committed value. It clears a marker only if that marker still names the projected version.

Cloudflare retries failed alarms six times, starting with two seconds of backoff.
After the final failed retry, the [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) schedules a fresh alarm at the next exponential backoff step.
An internal redrive method also supports operator recovery.

Readers never call the Durable Object.
They read KV and apply the declaration's `failurePolicy`.
The default policy uses the catalog default when the replica fails.

Under the default policy, an empty KV keeps deployment Config or declared defaults.
The `refuse` policy returns a typed failure on a missing or failed replica read.

The explicit KV ledger fallback retains attempt and outcome keys.
It refuses authoritative writes by default.
A composition must explicitly accept non-atomic writes without cross-isolate serialization.

## Keep urgent stops out of KV

KV changes can take 60 seconds or more to reach another location.
`off` bypasses approval, but it cannot bypass that propagation delay.
Use deployment settings or a stronger control path for kill switches and safety stops.

Each declaration also names its purpose, owner, and removal date.
The removal-date [fence](/lore/the-fence) keeps temporary flags from becoming forgotten behavior.

Use [Add a feature flag](/skills/add-a-feature-flag) to declare the ladder and its checks.
The generated models exercise scoped flips, denied approval, downward moves, attempt accounting, and projection recovery.
